## 1. Authority, dependencies, and exact new quantity

The finite geometric results concern any finite unit-separated planar source
`A`, of diameter `D>=1`, and its actual diameter endpoints `E`. Every original
point, including every contact isolate, enters the source upper constraints.
Global optimality, positive stress, near-disk geometry, a lattice, and train
extraction are not hypotheses of these results.

Canonical interpretation uses [Theory 01, Lemma 5.1](../theory/01-local-structure.md)
(actual diameter endpoints are extreme), [Target C 56](../targets/target-c/56-ci-restored-frontier.md)
(lexicographic full support), and [Target C 57](../targets/target-c/57-zr-zf-rigidity-audit.md)
(core-only rigidity and the loaded diameter ledger). They are not needed as
unproved steps in the elementary geometry below. No literature result is imported.

The unpromoted [transverse rail saturation](target_c_exact_phase_threshold_transverse_rail_saturation_2026-10-01.md)
supplies, within its hypotheses, two actual loaded endpoints for each internal
layer. It explicitly does **not** identify them with grid vertices or train
terminals. The application here is conditional on that report and on a further
physical extension hypothesis stated in Section 5.

Two related packets are acknowledged without importing their theorems:

- [Straight-row depth and populated rail caps](target_c_threshold_rail_deep_attachment_2026-10-02.md)
  uses a single affine marker row. Here the marker sequence can be any curve;
  the constraint couples its two translated copies.
- [Ball-hull lattice capacity](target_c_diameter_ball_hull_lattice_discrepancy_2026-10-02.md)
  controls endpoints near one unit-rhombic lattice. Here there is no lattice;
  the new quantity is `|(q_i-p_i)-w|` for the same actual paired endpoints.

## 2. Exact repeated displacement

**Theorem TR.1.** For every nonzero vector `w`, there are at most two distinct
points `p` such that both `p` and `p+w` are actual diameter endpoints of `A`.

**Proof.** Form the actual source ball hull

\[
 W=\bigcap_{a\in A}\overline B(a,D).
 \tag{TR.1}
\]

It contains `A`. Each `p in E` has an original partner `a` with `|p-a|=D`,
so lies on `partial W`. The body has interior: if `z,a` is a diameter pair
and `o=(z+a)/2`, the parallelogram identity gives
`|x-o|<=sqrt(3)D/2` for every `x in A`; hence
`B(o,(1-sqrt(3)/2)D) subset W`.

Rotate coordinates so `w=(t,0)`, `t>0`. At every interior height `y` in the
vertical projection of `W`, its horizontal slice is `[g(y),f(y)]`. Explicitly,

\[
\begin{split}
 f(y)&=\min_{a\in A}\{a_x+\sqrt{D^2-(y-a_y)^2}\},\\
 g(y)&=\max_{a\in A}\{a_x-\sqrt{D^2-(y-a_y)^2}\}.
\end{split}
\tag{TR.2}
\]

For a single circle the upper second derivative is
`-D^2/(D^2-(y-a_y)^2)^(3/2)<=-1/D`. Thus
`f(y)+y^2/(2D)` is concave: it is a minimum of concave functions.
Similarly `g(y)-y^2/(2D)` is convex. Consequently the slice width

\[
 \ell(y)=f(y)-g(y),\qquad \ell(y)+y^2/D\text{ is concave}.
 \tag{TR.3}
\]

In particular `ell` is strictly concave. Each horizontal slice has only two
boundary points, since `W` is strictly convex. The latter follows directly:
a strict convex combination of two distinct points of `W` is strictly inside
each of its finitely many defining disks, hence inside `W`.
If `p,p+w in partial W`, they are the left and right slice endpoints,
and `ell(p_y)=t`. A positive horizontal chord cannot occur at either extreme
height. A strictly concave width is strictly increasing to its unique maximum
and strictly decreasing afterwards; the equation `ell=t` has at most two
solutions. Each height has one left endpoint. This proves the claim. ∎

This exact consequence is also consistent with elementary convex-position
difference geometry. The load-bearing refinement below is the **quantitative**
source curvature in (TR.3), not a claimed contradiction from convexity alone.

**Corollary TR.2 (arbitrary additive charts).** Suppose an actual source contains
chart vertices `x_ij=a_i+b_j`, with the `a_i` distinct. Fix two distinct column
positions `b_j,b_k`. At most two rows can have both `x_ij` and `x_ik` actual
diameter endpoints. In particular a rectangular additive chart cannot have
three doubly loaded rows with the same opposite-column displacement.

This requires neither constant phases nor lattice coordinates. It does require
the two original loaded endpoints to be those chart vertices.

## 3. Approximate displacement capacity

**Theorem TR.3.** Fix `0<t=|w|<=D`, put `delta=t/D`, and let `epsilon>=0`.
Let `p_1,...,p_N` be distinct actual diameter endpoints. Suppose each has an
actual diameter endpoint `q_i` satisfying

\[
 |q_i-p_i-w|\le\epsilon.
 \tag{TR.4}
\]

The `q_i` need not be distinct, and need not be the diameter partners of the
`p_i`. Then

\[
 \boxed{N\le32\delta^{-3/2}\sqrt{D\epsilon}
                 +10\delta^{-1}\epsilon+4.}
 \tag{TR.5}
\]

For `epsilon=0`, TR.1 gives the sharper `N<=2`.

### Proof: original partner normals and bounded graph slopes

Use the coordinates of Section 2. If `p` has actual partner `a`, the unit
normal `n=(p-a)/D` satisfies, for every source point `x`,

\[
 n\cdot(x-p)\le-|x-p|^2/(2D).
 \tag{TR.6}
\]

It is also a supporting normal of `W`, since `W subset B(a,D)`.
First suppose `epsilon<=t^2/(16D)`. Set `r_i=q_i-p_i=w+e_i`.
Applying (TR.6) at `p_i` to `q_i`, and at `q_i` to `p_i`, gives

\[
 \begin{split}
 t(n_{p_i})_x&\le-(t-\epsilon)^2/(2D)+\epsilon
                       \le-3t^2/(8D),\\
 t(n_{q_i})_x&\ge (t-\epsilon)^2/(2D)-\epsilon
                       \ge 3t^2/(8D).
 \end{split}
 \tag{TR.7}
\]

Here `t<=D`, `epsilon<t`, and `t epsilon/D+epsilon<=2epsilon`
justify the last inequalities. In particular `p_i` is on the left graph,
`q_i` on the right graph, and their available supporting normals have
horizontal components of magnitude at least `delta/4`.
The corresponding subgradient of `g` or supergradient of `f` is
`-n_y/n_x`, with absolute value at most `4/delta`.

Let `I_p` be the interval between the smallest and largest `p_i` heights,
and define `I_q` similarly. Convex subgradients and concave supergradients
are ordered. Therefore `g` is `4/delta`-Lipschitz throughout `I_p`, and
`f` is `4/delta`-Lipschitz throughout `I_q`, even across corners.
For example every secant slope of `g` lies between an available subgradient
at the first point and one at the last point. This justifies the assertion
without smoothness or a bound on the number of defining disks.
Arclength of the left graph over an interval of height length `b` is at most

\[
 \sqrt{1+16/\delta^2}\,b\le(5/\delta)b.
 \tag{TR.8}
\]

### Proof: two short height packets

Since `|(p_i)_y-(q_i)_y|<=epsilon`, those `p_i` outside `I_q` occupy two
end intervals of height length at most `epsilon` each. Unit separation and
(TR.8) bound their number by `10 epsilon/delta+2`.

For the remaining points, both heights lie in `I_q`. Since `g((p_i)_y)=(p_i)_x`
and `f((q_i)_y)=(q_i)_x`, (TR.4) and the right-graph Lipschitz bound give

\[
 |\ell((p_i)_y)-t|\le\eta,
 \qquad \eta=(1+4/\delta)\epsilon.
 \tag{TR.9}
\]

On each side of the maximum of `ell`, the inverse image of
`[t-eta,t+eta]` is an interval of height length at most `sqrt(2D eta)`.
Indeed, for two heights `a<b` on the increasing side, strong concavity
(TR.3) and a nonnegative supergradient at `b` imply
`ell(b)-ell(a)>=(b-a)^2/D`; the difference is at most `2eta`.
On the decreasing side use a nonpositive supergradient at `a`.
Taking limits handles interval endpoints and corners.

Thus (TR.8) bounds the number of remaining unit-separated endpoints by
`10 sqrt(2D eta)/delta+2`. For every arc packet, `k` distinct source points
consume arclength at least `k-1`, since consecutive Euclidean chords are
at least one. Combining the packets, and using
`1+4/delta<=5/delta`, proves

\[
 N\le 10\sqrt{10}\,\delta^{-3/2}\sqrt{D\epsilon}
            +10\delta^{-1}\epsilon+4,
\]

which implies (TR.5), since `10 sqrt(10)<32`.

Finally, if `epsilon>t^2/(16D)`, the first term in (TR.5) is greater than
`8 D/sqrt(delta)>=8D`. The extreme-point lemma and unit separation give
`N<=pi D` when `N>=3`: the convex hull of these endpoints has `N` unit-separated
boundary sides and perimeter at most `pi D`. For `N<=2` the constant four
suffices. Thus (TR.5) also holds in this regime. ∎

**Corollary TR.4.** If `delta>=delta_0>0`, `N>=cD`, and `D` tends to
infinity, the displacement error cannot be `o(D)`. This is uniform in the
shape, population and phase of the source. It is an obstruction to actual
paired endpoints having a common macroscopic displacement, not an operator
estimate or a finite deformation.

## 4. Quadratic contact extension cost for arbitrary curved rows

The next finite consequence makes the source quantity explicit.

**Theorem TR.5.** Fix `c>0`, `0<delta_0<=1`, and put

\[
 \kappa=\min\{c^2\delta_0^3/65536,\ c\delta_0/160\}.
 \tag{TR.10}
\]

Suppose `D>=32/c`, there are `m>=cD` distinct actual endpoints `p_i`,
and actual endpoints `q_i`, with `delta_0 D<=|w|<=D`. Write

\[
 p_i=a_i+\xi_i,\qquad q_i=a_i+w+\zeta_i.
 \tag{TR.11}
\]

The markers `a_i` may form an arbitrary curve and need not be source points.
More than `m/2` of the indices obey

\[
 |\zeta_i-\xi_i|>\kappa D,
 \qquad |\xi_i|+|\zeta_i|>\kappa D.
 \tag{TR.12}
\]

Consequently

\[
 \boxed{\sum_i(|\xi_i|+|\zeta_i|)> c\kappa D^2/2.}
 \tag{TR.13}
\]

**Proof.** Apply (TR.5) to the subpacket with error at most `kappa D`.
Its count is at most

\[
 32\delta_0^{-3/2}\sqrt\kappa D+
 10\delta_0^{-1}\kappa D+4
 \le(c/8+c/16+c/8)D=5cD/16<m/2.
\]

Subtract from `m`, then use the triangle inequality and sum. ∎

If the markers are actual contact-chart vertices and the two offsets are
realized by paths of unit contacts, their path lengths `L_i^-`,`L_i^+`
satisfy `L_i^-+L_i^+>=|xi_i|+|zeta_i|`. Their **sum**, with path multiplicity,
therefore exceeds `c kappa D^2/2`. This is not a lower bound on distinct
contact edges: paths can share arbitrarily many edges.

In a complete additive contact chart, the two marker rows have
`a_i=A_i+B_j` and `a_i+w=A_i+B_k`, where `w=B_k-B_j`. The `A_i`
can be partial sums of different unit phase vectors. The theorem applies
without flattening that row or assuming an affine lattice.

## 5. Next source attack, and the exact surviving obstruction

Under the unpromoted threshold rail saturation, select one actual endpoint
on each transverse extreme rail for each internal layer. These are paired
by the layer index, not by an assertion that they form a diameter pair.
The new theorem would exclude the threshold branch if a linear subpacket
could be assigned to two opposite actual additive marker rows with

\[
 |w|\ge\delta_0D,\qquad
 \sum_i(L_i^-+L_i^+)=o(D^2).
 \tag{TR.14}
\]

The implication to contradiction is exact: use TR.5. The hypotheses in
(TR.14) are **not** supplied by a complement-dual layer label. The attempt
to import them from those labels fails at the physical co-embedding step.
Existing rail saturation explicitly warns that the selected endpoints need
not be grid vertices or train terminals. No such identification is made here.

A second attempted payment is also insufficient: rhombic source paths can
have `Theta(D)` length at `Theta(D)` different rows while the full source
has `Theta(D^2)` contact edges. Thus the quadratic cost (TR.13) does not
contradict an ordinary source-size bound. To close this mechanism one needs
an actual subquadratic extension budget, bounded congestion into a
subquadratic defect network, or a feasible finite source replacement.

Sections 6 and 7 test two hypotheses that cannot be discarded to evade this
obstruction. Neither regression is a global-minimizer family.

## 6. Regression: convex hull vertices cannot replace actual endpoints

Fix an integer `L>=2` and `e=1/100`. For `i=-L,...,L`, let

\[
 p_i=(i,L/2+e(1-i^2/L^2)),\qquad
 q_i=(i,-L/2-e(1-i^2/L^2)).
 \tag{TR.15}
\]

These are all strict convex-hull vertices; the upper parabola is strictly
concave and the lower parabola strictly convex. Points on the same row
have distinct integer horizontal coordinates and hence are at least unit
separated; the two rows have vertical separation at least `L`.

The actual diameter is `D=sqrt(5)L`, attained at opposite corner points.
For a cross-row pair put `x=i/L`, `y=j/L`, `k=2-x^2-y^2 in [0,2]`.
Since `(x-y)^2<=4-2k`,

\[
 |p_i-q_j|^2\le5L^2+k(-2L^2+2Le+2e^2)\le5L^2.
 \tag{TR.16}
\]

Same-row pairs have squared distance at most `4L^2+e^2<5L^2`.
Equality in the cross-row bound requires `k=0` and opposite horizontal
corners. Thus the interior row vertices are not diameter endpoints.

Nevertheless all `2L+1` ordered pairs satisfy
`|q_i-p_i-(0,-L)|<=2e=1/50`, and `|(0,-L)|/D=1/sqrt(5)` is macroscopic.
Their count grows linearly in `D`, exceeding (TR.5)'s square-root bound.
For example `L=1000`: use `D<3L`, `delta>1/3`; the right side of (TR.5)
is less than `192 sqrt(60)+1+4<1541`, while there are `2001` pairs.

The contact graph consists only of possible same-row edges and is triangle-free.
This is an exact regression against replacing actual diameter endpoints by
extreme source-hull vertices in the quantitative theorem.

## 7. Regression: the macroscopic displacement hypothesis is necessary

For an integer `R>=300`, put `J=floor(R/300)` and

\[
 z(s)=R\left({1-s^2\over1+s^2},{2s\over1+s^2}\right),
 \qquad p_i=z(3i/R),\quad q_i=z((3i+1)/R),\quad |i|\le J.
 \tag{TR.17}
\]

Take all these points and their antipodes as the source. All are on the
radius-`R` circle, so its diameter is exactly `D=2R` and every point is
an actual diameter endpoint. For these parameters `|s|<=1/50`. The identity

\[
 |z(s)-z(t)|^2={4R^2(s-t)^2\over(1+s^2)(1+t^2)}
 \tag{TR.18}
\]

gives strict unit separation within a parameter packet, since distinct
parameters differ by at least `1/R`. Antipodal packets are separated by
more than one as well. Thus the source is contact-free and triangle-free.

With `w=(0,2)`, the displacement errors have norm less than `1/10`.
Indeed `|z_x'(s)|/R<=4|s|` and
`0<=2-z_y'(s)/R<=6s^2`; integrate across a parameter interval of length
`1/R` to bound the sum of absolute coordinate errors by
`4/50+6/2500<1/10`.
There are `2J+1=Theta(D)` such pairs, while `|w|/D=1/R` tends to zero.
This rejects a bound `O(sqrt(D epsilon)+epsilon+1)` with no dependence
on the relative displacement. It does not contradict (TR.5).

These sources have linear population and no positive contact stress or
global optimality. No claim about dense canonical sources is inferred.

## 8. Computation and audit boundary

Run [the exact verifier](../../research/verify_translated_endpoint_rails_2026_10_02.py):

```text
python3 research/verify_translated_endpoint_rails_2026_10_02.py
```

It uses integer and Fraction arithmetic. It checks complete finite circle
sources and their actual diameter sets, repeated exact differences, original
partner support inequalities, approximate short-displacement packets, complete
small parabola sources, and the symbolic nonpositive coefficient used for the
large parabola regression. The general graph-width and capacity proofs above
are analytic; sampled survival is not their proof. Scope is also recorded in
[research/README](../../research/README.md).

| Claim or attempted extension | Classification | Exact boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Repeated endpoint displacement TR.1 | proved candidate awaiting promotion | Actual endpoint subset of one original source |
| Approximate capacity TR.3 | proved candidate awaiting promotion | Unit separation; constants depend on `delta` |
| Curved-row extension cost TR.5 | proved candidate awaiting promotion | Same actual paired endpoints and common `w` |
| Threshold exclusion using (TR.14) | conditional | Physical chart attachment and subquadratic cost unproved |
| Replace actual endpoints by convex-hull vertices | rejected / regression | (TR.15)–(TR.16) |
| Omit macroscopic-displacement dependence | rejected / regression | (TR.17)–(TR.18) |
| Pay path sum by ordinary quadratic contact population | rejected mechanism | Quadratic cost is compatible with quadratic source size |
| C-II or Level I closure | not proved | Surviving global branches remain |

The theorem permits stars, paths, shared endpoint partners, source isolates,
arbitrarily many ball-hull corners, and nonlattice phases. No isolated point
is dropped, no inactive pair is made active, no infinitesimal move is called
finite, and no diameter Euler path is used.

## 9. Frontier effect and next obligation

This is a physical paired-rail obstruction valid before KKT or extraction.
A linear family of actual loaded pairs cannot follow two macroscopic translates
of **any** marker curve at sublinear displacement error. If actual chart tails
exist, their total length is quadratic. This extends the single straight-row
depth mechanism to paired curved rows, without manufacturing a lattice atlas.

The concrete remaining theorem for this mechanism is to construct the physical
attachment in (TR.14), or charge its unavoidable quadratic cost to a genuinely
subquadratic source defect network with controlled path multiplicity. Constant
layer labels, positive cut reactions, and an ordinary `O(D^2)` contact bound
do not supply that theorem.

Open-cell exact phase, threshold staircase, nonconstant-phase swaps, split-star
O–O, inactive-interface, higher-surplus full-core, and macroscopic inactive
container/exterior-cap branches remain open. Canonical state and prompts are
unchanged. **Promotion recommendation: unreviewed**, with an independent audit
of the original-partner normals, nonsmooth slope bounds and level-band estimate
before any promotion transaction.
